  "derivation_reasoning": "Empirical measurements on this 15.63 GiB laptop show three distinct regimes: (1) Pandas hits RAM exhaustion (~5.5x expansion) above ~5M rows (~0.6 GB Snappy Parquet), failing at 5x (8.41M rows, needing 9.31 GB); (2) Polars Lazy handles 10x (16.83M rows, 2.03 GB Snappy Parquet) in 39.77s at 8.36 GB Peak RSS (~4.1x Parquet expansion due to full-panel sort + over(CERT) window frames), with latency bending super-linearly between 5x (8.02s) and 10x (39.77s) as OS virtual-memory paging begins near ~2.36 GB Parquet (~19.6M rows); (3) Scaling vertically to a 128 GB workstation extends single-node Polars to ~23.4 GB Parquet (~194.0M rows), beyond which (e.g., granular loan-level credit registry feeds) a multi-node PySpark cluster is objectively justified.",
